<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Adwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Adwa · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war">First Italo-Ethiopian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Adwa was fought in 1896 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> between the forces of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> and an invading Italian army. The Ethiopians won a decisive victory, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to recognise Ethiopian independence. It was a major setback for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> and became a symbol of African resistance to colonialism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa.jpg" alt="Battle of Adwa" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franko Khoury. · PDM-owner · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Adwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1889 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> with Menelik of Shewa, after occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. The treaty recognised Italy's claim to the coastal colony and promised financial help and military supplies. A dispute arose over its two versions. In the Italian text, Article 17 obliged the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to conduct all foreign affairs through Italian authorities, making Ethiopia a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> text said only that he could use Italy's good offices if he wished. Italian diplomats claimed that Menelik had knowingly signed a modified copy. Italy tried to force its reading on Ethiopia, and Menelik mobilised a large army, estimated at between 73,000 and over 100,000 men, mostly with modern rifles imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Italian army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri">Oreste Baratieri</a>, of between 14,519 and 17,770 men by modern estimates, was unfamiliar with the terrain and had poor reconnaissance. Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to act decisively, Baratieri ordered a surprise attack on the Ethiopian camp near Adwa. At about 9 p.m. the night before the battle, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> under Generals Matteo Albertone, Giuseppe Arimondi and Vittorio Dabormida marched towards the Ethiopian positions in three columns, with Giuseppe Ellena's brigade following in reserve. The fighting took place on 1 March 1896.</p><p>Albertone's vanguard was charged around 6 a.m. by Ethiopian units, and his brigade was routed, with two mountain batteries captured. Dabormida, trying to help, moved into the valley of Mariam Shewito, away from the rest of the army, and a gap of about two miles opened. Ras Makonnen pushed into it and cut Dabormida off. The Ethiopians then attacked Arimondi's centre, and a column of 25,000 men outflanked its right. The columns were isolated from one another, and the Italian positions were overwhelmed by sustained, numerically superior assaults.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of Italian dead run from about 5,900 to 6,133, and between 3,800 and 4,000 men were taken prisoner. Ethiopian losses are put at between 3,886 and 7,000 killed and 6,000 to 10,000 wounded. The Italians abandoned all their artillery and 11,000 rifles. Menelik did not pursue them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. His army was short of rations, the rains were near, and he feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> would send a larger force. He asked little beyond the abrogation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a>, since his crucial aim was to preserve independence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> recognised Ethiopian sovereignty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a>, and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a> fell. Adwa became a rallying point for later African nationalists and the Pan-African movement, and it left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> with a unique position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Some historians trace part of the roots of Italian Fascism to the wish to avenge the defeat, and in May 1936, when Italian troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> declared Adua è vendicata, Adwa has been avenged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Adwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's greatest victory came on 1 March 1896, when Menelik II's army of approximately 100,000 routed General Baratieri's Italian force at <b>Adwa</b>, killing over 6,000 Italian and colonial troops and capturing some 3,000 more.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion soon collided with the independent Ethiopian Empire, and it ended in defeat at <b>Adwa</b> in 1896, where Emperor Menelik II routed the army of General Oreste Baratieri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial expansion followed, with Eritrea organised as a colony in 1890 and a protectorate over much of Somalia, but the attempt to conquer Ethiopia ended in defeat at the <b>Battle of Adwa</b> in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ethiopia’s victory over an Italian army at <b>Adwa</b> in 1896 made it a symbol of African freedom for Black people everywhere.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>An early line recalling the Italian defeat at <b>Adwa</b> in 1896, which the regime did not want brought to mind, was replaced by a more general one.</blockquote><a href="/songs/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera</a></li><li><blockquote>It became the base for Italian ambitions in the Horn of Africa, checked at <b>Adwa</b> in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
